# Unit 7 – The Sermon at Benares : వర్క్‌బుక్ జవాబులు
SEC = [
 (97, "Let's Begin 1 – meanings of 'sermon'", [
  '(a) The text belongs to the first context – a talk on a religious/moral subject (the Buddha\'s teaching on death and grief).',
  '(b) Religious/moral talk: a speech by a saint, a pravachanam in a temple, a church sermon, a Friday khutba. | Admonition/lecture: parents warning us about mobile phones, a teacher scolding a late-comer, a coach\'s lecture after losing a match.',
  '(c) We say "Oh, not again!" when elders repeat the same advice many times – e.g., "study hard", "don\'t watch TV" – because we feel bored or think we already know it.']),
 (98, "Let's Begin 2 – 'Sermon at Benares' and 'Death Is Nothing At All'", [
  'Similarities: both are sermons by religious teachers about death; both console the grieving; both say death is natural and grief should not overpower us.',
  'Differences: The Buddha teaches that death is common to all, so one should accept it and stop lamenting to attain peace (detachment). Holland\'s poem says death is only "slipping into the next room" – the dead person is still near, love continues and we will meet again (continuity of life, hope of reunion). The Buddha uses a story (Kisa Gotami); the poem is a direct speech by the dead person.']),
 (99, 'Reading I – The Dalai Lama (Q1–Q4)', [
  'Q1 We must tackle the root of the problem, which lies in the human mind.',
  'Q2 The ultimate cause lies within the human mind.',
  'Q3 "It" refers to intelligence (accompanied by human affection and compassion).',
  'Q4 Because every human action, verbal or physical, even minor, has some motivation; proper motivation leads to good actions.']),
 (100, 'Reading I – Q5–Q7', [
  'Q5 Because if society\'s moral values become negative, every individual suffers; the intentions of each person shape the interests of society.',
  'Q6 Not a religious sense, but simply having a good and compassionate heart.',
  'Q7 (a) inner strength (b) less fear and less doubt (c) happiness and open-mindedness – more friends in society']),
 (102, 'Reading II – The Ballad of Father Gilligan', [
  'Q1 In his tiredness he had complained that he had no rest, joy or peace because people kept dying. He immediately felt sorry and asked God to forgive him – it was his tired body that complained, not his true self.',
  'Q2 (b) the passage of time',
  'Q3 He rode very fast without caring for the rocky lanes, marshes or his own safety and comfort, because he wanted to reach the dying man quickly. It does not mean he did not care for others; he did not care about the danger or the hardship of the road.',
  'Q4 (c) He was full of grief and self-condemnation.',
  'Q5 The wife said that the priest had already come and the man had died happily. The priest realised that God had sent an angel in his form while he slept; so he knelt down to thank God.',
  'Q6 God, who controls the planets and stars, had pity on the tired old priest asleep on a chair and sent an angel to comfort the dying man in his place.']),
 (103, 'Vocabulary – antonyms and synonyms', [
  '1. (a) befitted (b) grief / sorrow (c) lamentation (d) lost (e) hopeless / weary (f) dead (g) troubled',
  '2. (a) vow – promise, pledge, oath, swear | (b) preach – teach, sermonise, advocate, proclaim | (c) preserve – protect, conserve, save, maintain | (d) awaken – wake, rouse, arouse, stir | (e) enlighten – inform, educate, instruct, illuminate | (f) reign – rule, govern, command, prevail']),
 (104, 'Grammar 1 – Kisa Gotami in reported speech', [
  'Kisa Gotami had an only son and he died. In her grief she carried the dead child to all her neighbours and asked them for medicine. The people said that she had lost her senses and that the boy was dead.',
  'At length, Kisa Gotami met a man who replied to her request that he could not give her medicine for her child, but he knew a physician who could. The girl requested him to tell her who it was. The man told her to go to Sakyamuni, the Buddha.',
  'Kisa Gotami went to the Buddha and cried to him, addressing him as Lord and Master, to give her the medicine that would cure her boy. The Buddha answered that he wanted a handful of mustard-seed. When the girl joyfully promised to get it, the Buddha added that the mustard-seed must be taken from a house where no one had lost a child, husband, parent or friend.',
  'Poor Kisa Gotami went from house to house. The people pitied her and told her to take the mustard-seed. But when she asked whether a son or daughter, a father or mother had died in their family, they answered sadly that the living were few but the dead were many, and requested her not to remind them of their deepest grief.']),
 (105, 'Grammar 2 – The crow and the peacock (reported speech)', [
  'It is said that it is not how much we have, but how much we enjoy that makes happiness. … One day a crow saw a swan. He thought that the swan was so white and he was so black, and that the swan must be the happiest bird in the world.',
  'The swan replied that he had been feeling that he was the happiest bird around, but he thought the parrot was the happiest bird as it had two colours. The crow then approached the parrot. The parrot said that it had only two colours, but the peacock had multiple colours.',
  'The crow then visited a peacock in the zoo. Addressing him as dear peacock, the crow told him that he was so beautiful, that thousands of people came to see him every day, and that he was the happiest bird on the planet.',
  'The peacock said that he had always thought that he was the most beautiful and happy bird on the planet, but he was entrapped in the zoo. He added that he had realised that the crow was the only bird not kept in a cage, and could happily roam everywhere.']),
 (106, 'Grammar 3 – At the health clinic', [
  'Ashish complained that he felt sleepy all the time.',
  'Neha complained that she got pain in her legs when she ran.',
  'Sonal said that she was fine.',
  'Ritu mentioned that she had had fever the previous week.',
  'Akash asked why he always coughed.',
  'Rehman asked (the doctor) whether he should walk every day to keep himself fit.',
  'Sumi thanked the doctor and said that now she knew the reason for her constant stomach-ache.']),
 (107, 'Grammar 4 – said / told / asked', [
  '(a) said (b) told (c) tell (d) asked (e) tell (f) asked']),
 (108, 'Editing – The Wild Geese', [
  'One day, Prince Siddhartha was going through the royal gardens on his way to the river. A flock of wild geese passed overhead. Devadatta, the Prince\'s cousin, shot an arrow into their midst and one of the geese fell. It was wounded and fell just in front of Siddhartha. His heart was filled with compassion when he saw the poor bird bleeding profusely. He lifted it and drew the arrow very carefully from its body. He took the bird with him. Devadatta sent a messenger to claim the bird. But Siddhartha refused to give it. He said that it belonged to him because he had saved its life. It did not belong to Devadatta, who had tried to kill it.',
  'Words added: through, to, of, an, and, in, with, the, to, it, it']),
 (110, 'Listening – Sanchi Stupa', [
  '(a) The Sanchi Stupa (Great Stupa) in Madhya Pradesh; it portrays Buddhist art and sculpture.',
  '(b) National and foreign tourists, archaeologists and historians.',
  '(c) It was built during the Mauryan period; it was commissioned by Emperor Ashoka in the third century BCE.',
  '(d) The central chamber is inside the huge hemispherical dome; the relics of Lord Buddha are kept in it.',
  '(e) Four ornamental gateways (toranas) facing four directions and a balustrade surrounding the stupa.',
  '(f) (b) events of Lord Buddha\'s life [the Buddha is shown symbolically – (d) – on them too]']),
 (111, 'Speaking – grief and how to overcome it', [
  'Reasons: death of a family member or pet, failure in exams, illness or injury, losing a friend, moving to a new place, losing a position (head boy/girl, school team).',
  'Overcoming: talk to family and friends, express feelings (diary, art), keep a daily routine, play and exercise, help others, seek a counsellor or teacher if sadness continues, remember that loss is part of life (as the Buddha taught).']),
 (111, 'Writing 1 – Helping people in grief (paragraph)', [
  'People express grief in many ways – some cry, some become silent, some become angry. We should observe them carefully and understand that some may not want help or may not share their feelings at once; we should respect that. Others want to talk about their thoughts and memories; we must listen patiently without judging, and stand by them. Small actions – holding their hand, sitting with them, bringing food, helping with their work – give great comfort. We should let them know that we care and be patient, because healing takes time. Kind words and presence are often the best consolation.']),
 (112, 'Writing 2 – Summaries of the two poems', [
  'Kahlil Gibran – "Joy and Sorrow" (The Prophet): Joy and sorrow come from the same source; "your joy is your sorrow unmasked". The well that gives laughter was once filled with tears, the lute that soothes us was hollowed with knives, and the wine cup was burned in the oven. The deeper sorrow carves into us, the more joy we can hold. So the two are inseparable and we should accept both.',
  'Tagore: "Say not in grief that she is no more, but say in thankfulness that she was." We should not mourn a death but be thankful for the life that was lived. Death is not putting out the light of life; it is only putting out the lamp because the dawn (a new, higher life) has come.']),
 (113, 'Project – Jataka tales', [
  'Suggested tales: The Monkey King (Mahakapi), The Golden Deer, The Hare in the Moon (Sasa Jataka), The Tortoise and the Geese, The Wise Quail, The Elephant and the Dog. Rewrite each in your own words or as a comic strip, illustrate and display as a collage.']),
]
